A request graph $(G,R_=,R_{
eq},w)$ consists of a graph $G$, disjoint sets $R_=$ and $R_{
eq}$ of vertices of $G$ of degree two such that $R_=\cup R_{
eq}$ is an independent set, and positive rational weights $w$ on $R_=\cup R_{
eq}$ (the requests); it is planar triangle-free when $G$ is. In a proper $3$-coloring of $G$, a request $r\in R_=$ is satisfied if its two neighbours have the same colour and $r\in R_{
eq}$ is satisfied if they have different colours; the coloring satisfies an $\alpha$-fraction of the requests if the satisfied requests have total weight at least $\alpha\,w(R_=\cup R_{
eq})$. The problem arises from work of Asadi, Dvořák, Postle and Thomas. The authors prove that it has a positive answer if and only if Thomassen's conjecture holds, namely that there is $ eta>0$ such that every triangle-free planar graph $G$ has at least $2^{ eta|V(G)|}$ distinct $3$-colorings. They also show it suffices to consider only equality requests with unit weights, and they prove that $\alpha=1/5058$ works when all requests are non-equality requests attached to a common vertex. Known results on the conjecture: Thomassen proved it for planar graphs without $3$- and $4$-cycles, and a subexponential lower bound $2^{\sqrt{|V(G)|/212}}$ (Asadi, Dvořák, Postle and Thomas) holds for all triangle-free planar graphs; Dvořák and Lidický proved the bounded maximum degree case.
